        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-71910",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the laws of logarithms, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u20611-\\operatorname{ln}\u20613 \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{ln}\u20613 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}\u20613.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify our final answer as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}+C &=-\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20613}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      }
    ],
    ":knowledge-point/title": "Integrating an Exponential Function With a General Base"
  },
