      ":question/id": "89cd3b1f-2f20-5813-8f46-6c4d05bd7fcb",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-113893",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{x}}{ln\u2061\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the laws of logarithms, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ln\u2061\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right) & =ln\u20611-ln\u20618 \\\\  & =0-ln\u20618 \\\\  & =-ln\u20618.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify our final answer as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{x}}{ln\u2061\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}+C=-\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{x}}{ln\u20618}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
